================================================================================
WHAT THIS REDRAW FOUND
================================================================================

1. THE FRAME MOVED LESS THAN THE DRAW DID
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Rebuilt under npnormalize 0.1.9 against the same grant corpus and the same
seeded group draw (SEED 20261024), so only the match differs:

  identities   91,690 -> 91,008   (-682, duplicate identities merged)
  YES          41,256 -> 41,341   45.00% -> 45.43%   +0.43 pts
  MAYBE        24,107 -> 23,340   26.29% -> 25.65%   -0.64 pts
  NO           26,327 -> 26,327   28.71% -> 28.93%   +0.22 pts

Joined on the normalized recipient name -- see point 2 for why not on the id --
85,350 identities are in both runs and the tier is unchanged on 97.5%:

              new MAYBE     new NO    new YES
  old MAYBE       20,177        591        290
  old NO             418     24,440        415
  old YES            346         37     38,636

NO -> YES is 415 against 37 the other way, about 11:1, which is the real gain.
The NO total is unchanged to the identity, and that is consistent with the
residual being a blocking/scoring failure rather than a normalization one.

The pass mix moved more than the tiers. B1-token-state -- the weakest evidence
of the lot -- fell 40,504 -> 38,035 while A1-name-zip5 rose 26,289 -> 26,968,
A2-name-state 8,901 -> 9,636, D1-acronym 2,156 -> 2,792 and C1-name-any
3,515 -> 4,046. Work moving from the token pass to the name passes is the
direction a better normalizer should push it.

C2-dba-any is the exception and is worth a look of its own: 1,457 -> 458
overall, and 1,384 MAYBE -> 0. A pass that produced 1,384 ambiguous verdicts
and now produces none, while still firing 458 times, is either a real
improvement or a pass that stopped reaching its candidates. It took 23 of the
400 cases in the Nov-2 draw and takes none now, so the round no longer covers
it at all. NOT DIAGNOSED HERE.

2. grant_id IS POSITIONAL IN THE RUN, AND THE POPULATION CANNOT SEE IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The serious finding, and it nearly produced a false one.

Identity ids are R<k>, where k is the recipient's position among the
DEDUPLICATED recipients. Merging 682 duplicates renumbered everything after the
first merge. Joined on grant_id across the two runs the recipient names do not
agree at all:

  R10000   Childrens Guardian                   CONGREGATION ATERES YESHAYA
  R10001   Thoughts of Grace                    SPRINGFIELD YESHIVA
  R10002   Zion Lutheran Church                 YESHIVAH ATERES SHMUEL OF ...

Every id-joined comparison is therefore a comparison of unrelated rows, and it
does not look like noise -- it looks like a finding. Three false ones were drawn
and discarded before the names were checked:

  "6 of 400 cases in common" -- 400/23,340 = 1.7%, i.e. chance.
  "182 of the old 400 are now YES, 112 NO, 103 MAYBE" -- the new frame's base
    rates (45.4/28.9/25.7%) on 400 random ids, to within four cases.
  "the old C2-dba-any MAYBEs went to A1 and B1" -- base rates again.

And the population fingerprint does not catch it: it is cb3c78ff0f1a9546 for
BOTH runs, correctly, because the grant file really is the same. So a label
drawn from the Oct-25 frame carries a population that validates against the
Nov-3 frame and attaches to a different organisation. That is the one failure
dev/TRUTH-LABELING-PROTOCOL.md section 4 tells labelers is impossible
("population is a 16-character fingerprint that makes these labels
re-attachable"), arriving on the axis nobody fingerprinted.

pf_frame_id() now fingerprints the id-to-name pairing, and pf_round_sample()
records it beside the population:

  Oct-25 frame   population cb3c78ff0f1a9546   frame_id 32563729181651de
  Nov-03 frame   population cb3c78ff0f1a9546   frame_id 72edafa8f2114af9

3. WHAT IS STILL OPEN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
pf_shard_schema() has no frame_id column, so a labeler's returned truth rows
carry the population and not the numbering. pf_read_truth() can therefore
enforce the fingerprint that does not catch this and not the one that does.
Dealing the shards before that is settled writes labeler-facing files that
would have to be re-dealt with overwrite = TRUE, so stage 1 is drawn and NOT
dealt.

Also undiagnosed: C2-dba-any (point 1).
